# DevTrack — Interview Explanation (STAR + Tech)

---

## ⚡ 30-Second Elevator Pitch

> "I built **DevTrack**, a developer productivity dashboard that solves the problem of developers having no data-driven way to measure their GitHub performance. I used **React.js, Node.js, Express, MongoDB, and the GitHub REST API** and was personally responsible for the entire full-stack development — the scoring algorithm, comparison engine, authentication, and deployment. The biggest challenge was designing a **fair scoring algorithm that works across developers with wildly different activity levels**, which I solved using **tanh-based normalization with dynamic benchmarks**. By the end, the app has a **multi-factor DevScore engine, a Developer Battle Arena for head-to-head comparison, JWT authentication, interactive Recharts visualizations**, and is **live on Vercel + Render** at `devtrack-242424.vercel.app`."

---

## 🔄 Complete Project Workflow (End-to-End)

### How the entire system works — from user to data:

```
User opens devtrack-242424.vercel.app
       ↓
Vercel serves React SPA (index.html + JS bundle)
       ↓
User sees Login/Signup screen
       ↓
POST /auth/register → bcrypt hashes password → saves to MongoDB Atlas
       ↓
POST /auth/login → bcrypt.compare() → jwt.sign() → returns JWT token
       ↓
Token stored in localStorage → user redirected to Dashboard
       ↓
React sets activeUser → triggers useEffect hook
       ↓
Two concurrent API calls fire via Promise.all:
  GET /github/:username  → raw GitHub profile + repos
  GET /analyze/:username → computed analysis + score
       ↓
Backend fetches from GitHub REST API (authenticated with token)
       ↓
Scoring algorithm computes DevScore (influence + activity + quality)
       ↓
Search saved to MongoDB (Search collection)
       ↓
JSON response sent back → React renders dashboard with Recharts
       ↓
Profile + Stats + DNA + Weakness + Score + Timeline + Repos + Skills ✅
```

### How a profile analysis flows through the backend:

```
React calls GET /analyze/torvalds
       ↓
Express routes to analyze.js router
       ↓
Promise.all fires 2 GitHub API calls concurrently:
  GET api.github.com/users/torvalds
  GET api.github.com/users/torvalds/repos
       ↓
GitHub returns user data + repos array
       ↓
utils/analyze.js processes the data:
  → getSkills(repos)        — extracts language map
  → getTopLanguage(skills)  — finds dominant language
  → getDNA(skills)          — classifies developer type
  → getWeakness(user, repos, benchmarks) — detects gaps
  → getInsights(user, repos, skills, benchmarks) — generates insights
  → getDevScore(user, repos, benchmarks) — calculates normalized score
       ↓
Search history saved to MongoDB (username + score + timestamp)
       ↓
JSON response with all analysis sent to React
```

### How the comparison engine works:

```
User enters "user1, user2, user3" on Compare page
       ↓
React sends POST /compare with { users: ["user1", "user2", "user3"] }
       ↓
STEP 1: Fetch all users concurrently (Promise.all)
  → 6 GitHub API calls (2 per user) run in parallel
       ↓
STEP 2: Calculate dynamic benchmarks from the group
  → avgFollowers, avgRepos, avgStars (relative to THIS group)
       ↓
STEP 3: Score each user using tanh normalization
  → influence (35%) + activity (25%) + quality (25%)
  → Math.tanh() keeps scores in 0-1 range
  → Math.log() for follower counts (logarithmic scaling)
       ↓
STEP 4: Classify each developer (type + hiring fit)
       ↓
STEP 5: Sort by score → generate leaderboard
       ↓
STEP 6: Save comparison to MongoDB (Comparison collection)
       ↓
STEP 7: Update Leaderboard collection (upsert pattern)
       ↓
STEP 8: Generate AI insight about the winner
       ↓
JSON response → React renders charts + leaderboard + skills comparison
```

### How authentication works:

```
User clicks "Signup"
       ↓
React sends POST /auth/register { email, password, githubUsername }
       ↓
Express checks if email already exists in MongoDB
       ↓
bcrypt.hash(password, 10) → 10 salt rounds → one-way hash
       ↓
User saved to MongoDB with hashed password
       ↓
User redirected to Login screen
       ↓
POST /auth/login { email, password }
       ↓
bcrypt.compare(password, storedHash) → true/false
       ↓
jwt.sign({ id: user._id }, JWT_SECRET, { expiresIn: "7d" })
       ↓
Token + githubUsername returned to React
       ↓
localStorage stores token → sent with every API request
```

