## 💡 Likely Follow-up Questions & Answers

**"What would you do differently if you rebuilt it?"**
> "Five things — I'd add **Redis caching** for GitHub API responses to reduce redundant calls, use **httpOnly cookies** instead of localStorage for JWT storage to prevent XSS, add **GitHub OAuth** so users can log in with their GitHub account, implement **rate limiting middleware** (express-rate-limit) to prevent API abuse, and add **Prometheus + Grafana** for monitoring."

**"How does your scoring algorithm work under the hood?"**
> "I evaluate developers on three dimensions: Influence (40% — followers normalized against max), Activity (30% — repo count normalized), and Quality (30% — multi-factor repo scoring: stars +2, forks +1, description +1, original work +1). Each metric is divided by the max value to normalize to 0–1, then multiplied by its weight. For the comparison engine, I use `Math.tanh()` instead of linear normalization because it produces a smooth S-curve — so extreme outliers like 50,000 followers don't compress everyone else to zero."

**"How would you scale this?"**
> "Three layers: (1) **Caching** — Redis for GitHub API responses with TTL-based expiry. A profile doesn't change every minute, so caching for 1 hour eliminates 80% of redundant API calls. (2) **Horizontal scaling** — the Express backend is stateless (JWT, no sessions), so I can run multiple instances behind a load balancer. (3) **Database** — MongoDB Atlas supports replica sets and sharding out of the box. (4) **CDN** — Vercel already serves the frontend from edge locations globally."

**"What was your biggest learning?"**
> "That the algorithm design mattered more than the tech stack. I went through three iterations of the scoring — first linear (broke with outliers), then percentage-based (unfair for small profiles), and finally tanh-based with logarithmic scaling. The lesson was that **data normalization is a real engineering problem**, not just a math concept."

**"Why not use a framework like Next.js?"**
> "DevTrack is a Single Page Application — all rendering happens client-side after login. There's no SEO requirement (it's a dashboard behind auth), no server-side rendering needed. Create React App gave me a fast development setup with zero config. Next.js would add complexity (file-based routing, API routes, SSR) without adding value for this use case."

**"Why MongoDB and not PostgreSQL?"**
> "The data structures are naturally document-oriented. A comparison result contains nested objects — an array of users, each with score breakdowns, skills maps, type classifications, and hiring suggestions. In PostgreSQL, this would need multiple tables and JOINs. In MongoDB, it's a single document with nested objects — simpler writes, simpler reads. The Leaderboard uses `findOneAndUpdate` with `upsert: true` — an atomic operation that either updates an existing record or creates a new one, which is perfect for a leaderboard pattern."

**"How does the comparison engine handle fairness?"**
> "The key insight is **dynamic benchmarks**. Instead of saying 'more than 100 repos = good', I calculate the average from the compared group itself. If you compare two beginners with 10 repos each, the benchmark is 10. If you add a senior dev with 500 repos, the benchmark shifts to ~173. Scores are always relative to the group, never absolute. This means a comparison between two students is just as meaningful as a comparison between senior engineers."

**"Explain your authentication flow"**
> "On registration, I hash the password with `bcrypt.hash(password, 10)` — 10 salt rounds means ~100ms per hash, slow enough to prevent brute force. On login, `bcrypt.compare()` checks the password against the stored hash. If it matches, `jwt.sign()` creates a token with the user's MongoDB `_id` and a 7-day expiry. The token is sent to the frontend, stored in `localStorage`, and included in the `Authorization` header of every subsequent API request."

