## 🌐 How Frontend & Backend Are Served (Separate Deployment)

### Why there ARE two separate servers:

Unlike the Smart Bug Tracker (where Spring Boot served everything on one port), DevTrack has a **decoupled architecture**:

| Component | Technology | Hosted On | URL |
|---|---|---|---|
| Frontend | React (Create React App) | Vercel | `devtrack-242424.vercel.app` |
| Backend | Node.js + Express | Render | `devtrack-backend-xmag.onrender.com` |
| Database | MongoDB | MongoDB Atlas | Cloud cluster |

### How they connect:

```
Step 1: User opens devtrack-242424.vercel.app
Step 2: Vercel serves the React SPA (index.html + JS bundle)
Step 3: React app loads in browser
Step 4: JavaScript calls the backend API at devtrack-backend-xmag.onrender.com
Step 5: Express backend fetches from GitHub API + computes analysis
Step 6: JSON response sent back to React (CORS enabled with origin: "*")
Step 7: React renders the data with Recharts visualizations
```

### Environment-aware API URL:

```javascript
const BASE_URL =
  process.env.NODE_ENV === "development"
    ? "http://localhost:5000"           // local dev
    : "https://devtrack-backend-xmag.onrender.com";  // production
```

> **Interview line**: "The frontend and backend are deployed independently — React on Vercel, Node.js on Render. In development, the React dev server proxies API calls to `localhost:5000`. In production, API calls go to the Render URL. CORS is configured on the backend to allow cross-origin requests."

